  <refmeta>
    <refentrytitle>ndm_decomposecollisions</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
  </refmeta>

  <refnamediv>
    <refname>ndm_decomposecollisions</refname>
    <refpurpose>
      Detect and decompose spike collision waveforms into single-unit components
    </refpurpose>
  </refnamediv>

  <refsynopsisdiv>
    <cmdsynopsis>
      <command>ndm_decomposecollisions</command>
      <arg choice="opt"><replaceable>session</replaceable></arg>
    </cmdsynopsis>
  </refsynopsisdiv>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Description</title>
    <para>
      <command>ndm_decomposecollisions</command> identifies spike waveforms
      that are composed of two overlapping action potentials ("collisions")
      and decomposes each collision into its constituent single-unit components.
      It operates post-curation: the curated
      <filename>.clu.N</filename> files provide the unit labels from which
      mean waveform templates are derived.  The original
      <filename>.clu.N</filename>, <filename>.res.N</filename>, and
      <filename>.spk.N</filename> files are never modified.
    </para>
    <para>
      When two neurons fire within approximately one spike-width of each other
      (roughly 0.5–1 ms at typical sampling rates), their extracellular
      waveforms sum linearly at each electrode site.  The composite waveform
      occupies an intermediate region of PCA feature space and is commonly
      misassigned — either to the dominant parent cluster or to the noise
      cluster — during automated sorting.  Even after careful manual curation,
      collision spikes inflate false-positive and false-negative firing rates
      and produce artifactual troughs in cross-correlograms near zero lag.
    </para>
    <para>
      All template matching is restricted to spikes within a single
      spikeDetection group (same tetrode or polytrode shank).  Cross-shank
      collisions are not attempted because the spatial overlap of waveforms
      from different shanks at individual electrode sites is negligible
      compared to the within-shank signal.
    </para>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Algorithm</title>

    <refsect2>
      <title>Stage 1 — Template building</title>
      <para>
        For each curated single unit (cluster ≥ 2 by default) that fired at
        least <parameter>minSpikesTemplate</parameter> spikes, the algorithm
        computes the mean waveform template
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>T_k</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        of shape (<emphasis>n_samples</emphasis> × <emphasis>n_sites</emphasis>)
        in floating point.  The per-unit peak-to-peak amplitude distribution
        on the dominant channel (the site with the largest mean PTP) is stored
        as 1st and 99th percentiles; these bounds are used in the acceptance
        gate at Stage 3.
      </para>
      <para>
        At least two qualifying templates must be present for a group to be
        processed.  Groups with only one unit cannot produce collisions between
        distinct neurons (same-unit burst doublets are handled separately via
        <parameter>maxShiftSamp</parameter>).
      </para>
    </refsect2>

    <refsect2>
      <title>Stage 2 — Single-template screening</title>
      <para>
        Every spike waveform <inlineequation><mathphrase>W</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        is correlated against all templates over a grid of integer shifts
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>τ ∈ [−maxShiftSamp, +maxShiftSamp]</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        samples.  The correlation measure is the Pearson r computed over all
        (sample × site) values in the overlapping region after applying the
        shift — no zero-padding is used.
      </para>
      <para>
        If the best-match Pearson r exceeds
        <parameter>corrThreshold</parameter>, the spike is well explained by a
        single template and is skipped.  Spikes whose peak-to-peak amplitude
        across all sites is below
        <parameter>minSnrRms</parameter> × estimated RMS noise are also
        skipped; they are too weak for reliable decomposition.  The noise
        floor is estimated from the full waveform matrix using the MAD
        estimator
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>σ ≈ median(|x|) / 0.6745</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        (Quiroga 2004), which is robust in the presence of spike signals.
      </para>
    </refsect2>

    <refsect2>
      <title>Stage 3 — Two-component matching pursuit</title>
      <para>
        For each collision candidate, two greedy passes find the (unit, shift,
        amplitude) triplets that minimise the residual waveform energy.
      </para>
      <para>
        <emphasis role="bold">Pass 1</emphasis> — find the dominant component:
        For each template <inlineequation><mathphrase>T_k</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        and shift <inlineequation><mathphrase>τ</mathphrase></inlineequation>,
        the amplitude
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>a = ⟨W, T_k(τ)⟩ / ‖T_k‖²</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        (the least-squares scalar) is computed and the residual
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>R₁ = W − a·T_k(τ)</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        is evaluated.  The (k₁, τ₁, a₁) with the smallest
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>‖R₁‖</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        is retained.
      </para>
      <para>
        <emphasis role="bold">Pass 2</emphasis> — fit the residual:
        The same scan is applied to <inlineequation><mathphrase>R₁</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        to find (k₂, τ₂, a₂).  The second unit may be the same as the first,
        capturing burst doublets from a single neuron.
      </para>
      <para>
        A decomposition is <emphasis>accepted</emphasis> when all three
        conditions hold:
      </para>
      <itemizedlist>
        <listitem><para>
          <emphasis>Residual gate</emphasis>:
          <inlineequation><mathphrase>‖R₂‖ / ‖W‖ &lt; residualThreshold</mathphrase></inlineequation>.
          The two-template model must account for at least
          (1 − <parameter>residualThreshold</parameter>) × 100 % of the
          waveform energy.
        </para></listitem>
        <listitem><para>
          <emphasis>Amplitude gate</emphasis>:
          Both fitted amplitudes a₁ and a₂ fall within the 1st–99th percentile
          of the respective unit's empirical amplitude distribution.  This
          rejects solutions where a component has a physically implausible
          amplitude (e.g., 3× the unit mean).
        </para></listitem>
        <listitem><para>
          <emphasis>Sign gate</emphasis>:
          Both amplitudes are positive.  Anti-phase solutions indicate a
          spurious template match and are rejected.
        </para></listitem>
      </itemizedlist>
      <para>
        After finding the integer-sample optimum τ, sub-sample precision is
        achieved by parabolic interpolation on the three adjacent residual
        values.  The refined fractional shift is stored in
        <structfield>shiftFrac</structfield> alongside the integer
        <structfield>shiftSamp</structfield>.
      </para>
    </refsect2>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Prerequisites</title>
    <itemizedlist>
      <listitem><para>
        Spike sorting must be complete and manual curation in
        <application>Klusters</application> must be finished.
        <filename>.clu.N</filename>, <filename>.res.N</filename>, and
        <filename>.spk.N</filename> files must exist for all groups.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        Each spikeDetection group must contain at least two curated single
        units with at least <parameter>minSpikesTemplate</parameter> spikes
        each.  Groups with only one unit are skipped.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        Python 3.10 or later with <package>pyyaml</package> and
        <package>numpy</package> installed.
        <package>scipy</package> is optional but not required.
      </para></listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Parameters</title>
    <variablelist>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>maxShiftSamp</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Maximum temporal shift (± samples) explored when matching templates
          to spike waveforms.  At 32 kHz, the default of 10 samples covers
          ±0.31 ms, which encompasses collisions between independently firing
          neurons.  Increase to 16 for lower sampling rates or broader
          waveforms; larger values increase runtime approximately as
          O(maxShiftSamp × n_templates).
          (Default: 10)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>corrThreshold</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Pearson r threshold for the single-template screening stage.
          Spikes with a best-match correlation ≥ this value are considered
          well explained by one unit and are not passed to the two-component
          pursuit.  Increase toward 0.92 to flag only the most obvious
          collisions; decrease toward 0.75 to widen the candidate pool
          (at the cost of more false positives in stage 3).
          (Default: 0.85)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>residualThreshold</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Maximum fractional residual energy
          <inlineequation><mathphrase>‖R₂‖ / ‖W‖</mathphrase></inlineequation>
          for a two-template decomposition to be accepted.  A value of 0.25
          means the model must account for ≥ 75 % of the waveform energy.
          Tighten to 0.15 for high-SNR recordings; relax to 0.35 when the
          noise floor is elevated.
          (Default: 0.25)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>minSnrRms</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Minimum spike amplitude in multiples of the estimated RMS noise
          floor.  Spikes below this threshold are skipped in the screening
          stage because their waveform SNR is insufficient for reliable
          decomposition.  The noise floor is estimated from all spike
          waveforms using the MAD estimator (Quiroga 2004).
          (Default: 4.0)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>minSpikesTemplate</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Minimum spikes a curated unit must have fired to be included in the
          template library.  Units with fewer spikes produce noisy mean
          waveforms that degrade decomposition accuracy.
          (Default: 30)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>excludeNoise</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          When <literal>true</literal>, cluster 0 (artifact/noise) and
          cluster 1 (MUA) are excluded from template building.  Their
          waveforms are too heterogeneous to serve as meaningful collision
          components.  Set to <literal>false</literal> only if MUA was
          intentionally curated into a homogeneous population.
          (Default: true)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>overwrite</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          When <literal>false</literal>, spikeDetection groups whose
          <filename>.col.N</filename> file already exists are skipped.
          Set to <literal>true</literal> to force recomputation after
          adjusting parameters.
          (Default: false)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Output File Format</title>
    <para>
      One <filename>SESSION.col.N</filename> file is written per spikeDetection
      group.  The file is a YAML document with the following structure:
    </para>
    <programlisting language="yaml"><![CDATA[
collisions:
  format: '1.0'
  session: jg05-20120316
  spikeGroup: 1
  nSpikes: 14302
  nTemplates: 6
  nCandidates: 847
  nDecomposed: 312
  rmsNoise: 48.3
  corrThreshold: 0.85
  residualThreshold: 0.25
  maxShiftSamp: 10
  minSnrRms: 4.0
  minSpikesTemplate: 30
  templates:
    - unitId: 2
      nSpikes: 1204
      dominantChannel: 3
      meanPtp: 142.3
      ampPct01: 98.4
      ampPct99: 201.7
    - unitId: 3
      nSpikes: 876
      dominantChannel: 1
      meanPtp: 87.1
      ampPct01: 61.2
      ampPct99: 118.5
  spikes:
    - spikeIndex: 47
      timestamp: 19423
      bestSingleUnit: 2
      bestSingleCorr: 0.781
      collision_accepted: true
      component1:
        unitId: 3
        shiftSamp: -4
        shiftFrac: -3.82
        amplitude: 1.03
        ampInRange: true
      component2:
        unitId: 2
        shiftSamp: 6
        shiftFrac: 6.14
        amplitude: 0.97
        ampInRange: true
      residualNorm: 0.19
    ]]></programlisting>

    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><structfield>spikeIndex</structfield></term>
        <listitem><para>
          0-based index into the <filename>.res.N</filename> /
          <filename>.clu.N</filename> / <filename>.spk.N</filename> arrays.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><structfield>timestamp</structfield></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spike detection time in samples (from <filename>.res.N</filename>).
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><structfield>collision_accepted</structfield></term>
        <listitem><para>
          <literal>true</literal> when all acceptance criteria are met.
          <literal>false</literal> when the spike is a candidate but the
          decomposition was not convincing; treat as unresolved.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><structfield>shiftSamp</structfield></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Integer-sample shift applied to the component template relative to
          the spike detection time.  Positive = the component template was
          shifted later (the component spike arrived earlier).
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><structfield>shiftFrac</structfield></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Sub-sample refined shift from parabolic interpolation.  Use this
          when constructing corrected spike times at sub-sample precision.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><structfield>amplitude</structfield></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Dimensionless scale factor relative to the mean template.
          A value of 1.0 means the component amplitude exactly matches the
          unit mean; values between 0.6 and 1.4 are typical for well-isolated
          units with moderate amplitude variability.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><structfield>residualNorm</structfield></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Fractional residual energy
          <inlineequation><mathphrase>‖R₂‖ / ‖W‖</mathphrase></inlineequation>
          after subtracting both components.  Lower is better; accepted
          decompositions have values below
          <parameter>residualThreshold</parameter>.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Downstream Use</title>
    <para>
      The <filename>.col.N</filename> sidecar provides two downstream options
      without altering the original spike-sorting files:
    </para>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Exclusion</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spikes with <structfield>collision_accepted == true</structfield>
          are removed from firing-rate computations and cross-correlograms.
          This is the conservative approach and is always safe.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Reassignment</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Each accepted collision spike at time <emphasis>t</emphasis> is
          replaced by two events: one for unit k₁ at
          <emphasis>t + shiftSamp_1</emphasis> (or
          <emphasis>t + shiftFrac_1</emphasis> for sub-sample precision) and
          one for unit k₂ at <emphasis>t + shiftSamp_2</emphasis>.  This
          recovers spikes that would otherwise be permanently lost from both
          units' spike trains.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
    <para>
      For cross-correlogram analysis, excluding accepted collisions recovers
      the true refractory dip between units and removes the spurious excess
      coincidences that confound functional connectivity inference.
    </para>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Limitations</title>
    <itemizedlist>
      <listitem><para>
        Three-component collisions (three neurons firing within a single
        waveform window) are not attempted.  They are rare (&lt; 0.1 % of
        spikes in typical recordings) and the two-pass pursuit will usually
        assign the pair with the highest combined energy, leaving a
        detectable residual.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        The algorithm assumes that within-collision amplitude variability
        follows the same distribution as single-unit amplitude variability.
        For units with very narrow amplitude distributions the acceptance gate
        may be over-restrictive; lowering the percentile bounds (via source
        modification) relaxes this.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        Template drift over the course of a long recording degrades
        decomposition accuracy.  Run
        <citerefentry>
          <refentrytitle>ndm_estimatedrift</refentrytitle>
          <manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
        </citerefentry>
        first and inspect the drift output.  Sessions with drift &gt; 10 µm/h
        should be corrected or split into shorter segments before collision
        decomposition.
      </para></listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Files</title>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.yaml</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Session parameter file (mandatory input).  The
          <literal>spikeDetection.channelGroups</literal> section is read
          for per-group <literal>nSamples</literal>,
          <literal>peakSampleIndex</literal>, and channel lists.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.clu.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Curated cluster labels (mandatory input).
          Must reflect post-curation single-unit assignments.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.res.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spike timestamps in samples (mandatory input).
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.spk.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spike waveforms — int16 binary, row-major (spike × sample × site)
          (mandatory input).  Waveform data is required; the plugin exits with
          an error if no <filename>.spk.N</filename> files are found.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.col.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Output collision sidecar file (YAML).  One per spikeDetection group.
          Contains global statistics, template metadata, and per-spike
          decomposition records.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

